  <h2>OTP Security Best Practices</h2>
  <p>
    OTP codes only help if they stay with you. A few precautions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never Share OTPs Over the Phone:</strong> Legitimate customer support representatives will never ask you to read out an authentication code or password reset token.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify the Sending Domain:</strong> Check the sender's domain, and make sure you're entering the code on the real site rather than a phishing copy.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Disposable Inboxes for Disposable Services:</strong> For temporary trials, forums, or downloads, rely on <a href="/blog/why-use-temporary-email/">disposable temporary email addresses</a> to keep your primary inbox uncluttered and private.</li>
    <li><strong>Reserve Dedicated Inboxes for Permanent Accounts:</strong> For critical accounts like banking or government portals, use your secure primary inbox backed by hardware security keys or authenticator apps. Review our <a href="/blog/email-privacy-best-practices/">email privacy best practices</a> for more strategies.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Conclusion</h2>
  <p>
    One-time codes are a simple, widely used way to prove you control an email address or device. Know where they come from, never pass one on to someone who asks for it, and keep codes for important accounts in your real inbox.
  </p>
  <p>
    To try it, open <a href="/">TempoEmails</a> and use the address in a signup form, or read our breakdown of <a href="/blog/how-disposable-email-protects-privacy/">how disposable email protects your online privacy</a>.
  </p>
